  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nEarly this afternoon, a batch of showers and storms continues to \nmove east-southeast into southeastern Nebraska along a theta-e and \ninstability gradient. There is a low chance those showers/storms \nclip far northeast KS this afternoon as that system moves east-\nsoutheast with the mean flow. Low (10-15%) chances remain in the \nforecast across the area this evening and tonight with the \npossibility of another weak wave within the northwest flow aloft. \nConfidence remains low in storms developing given a capped air mass \nand overall weak forcing. However, a stronger LLJ sets up tonight and\nmid-level lapse rates remain steep. If anything come overcome the \nlow level CIN, strong winds up to 60 mph will be possible with storms.\n\nFocus then shifts to the building heat. Temperatures at H85 warm to \nnear 30C and the southwestern H5 ridge expands slightly over the \narea. Combined with strong solar insolation, high temperatures are \ncurrently forecast to reach the upper 90s and low 100s -- the hottest\nconditions across central KS. Luckily, boundary layer mixing should \nmix down lower Td's during the afternoon. Still, heat index values \nare expected to range from about 102 to 106 degrees. Have continued \nto hold off on heat headlines with marginal advisory conditions in \nplace, but steps should still be taken to protect yourself from the \nheat.\n\nSimilar conditions stay in place through Tuesday of next week. The \nmain uncertainty remains on timing and placement of any weak \nshortwaves on the northern periphary of the mid-level ridge. The \nmost likely scenario would be for those to impact locations north of \nhere. Our environment will remain strongly capped given the very warm \nH85 and H7 temperatures and synoptic forcing should remain weak.  \n\nA pattern shift occurs during the middle of next week when the ridge \nflattens and moves southeast. Long range models, however, differ on \nthe strength of storm systems both to the northeast and northwest of \nthe region. Most models show a surface boundary in the area \nthroughout the second half of the week, which increases confidence is\nPOPs. NBM currently shows the highest POPs occurring Wednesday night\nand Thursday morning, and again Thursday night and Friday morning.",
